**Setup:** Draft samples x ~ q(x). Accept with probability min(1, p(x)/q(x)). If rejected, resample from the residual p'(x) = max(0, p(x)−q(x)) / Z, where Z = Σₓ max(0, p(x)−q(x)).

---

**Step 1 — Probability of proposing x *and* accepting it:**
$$P(\text{propose }x,\text{ accept}) = q(x)\cdot\min\left(1,\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}\right) = \min(p(x),q(x))$$
*(Intuition: you can never "accept" more of x than the smaller of what p wants and what q offers — that's the ceiling.)*

**Step 2 — Total accept/reject probability, summed over the whole vocabulary:**
$$P(\text{accept}) = \sum_x \min(p(x),q(x)), \qquad P(\text{reject}) = 1-\sum_x \min(p(x),q(x))$$
*(Just adding Step 1's per-token result across every token to get one overall number.)*

**Step 3 — Show the residual's normalizer Z equals P(reject):**
$$Z=\sum_x\max(0,p(x)-q(x))=\sum_x\big(p(x)-\min(p(x),q(x))\big)=1-\sum_x\min(p(x),q(x))=P(\text{reject})$$
*(Intuition: "total leftover mass p has that q didn't cover" and "how often we reject" are the same shortfall, just measured two different ways — so they're forced to be numerically equal.)*

**Step 4 — Total probability of ending on token x** (via acceptance OR via reject-then-resample):
$$P(\text{final}=x)=\min(p(x),q(x))+P(\text{reject})\cdot p'(x)=\min(p(x),q(x))+Z\cdot\frac{\max(0,p(x)-q(x))}{Z}$$
$$=\min(p(x),q(x))+\max(0,p(x)-q(x))$$
*(The Z's cancel — numerator and denominator — because of Step 3's identity. That cancellation is the crux of the whole proof.)*

**Step 5 — Case check, to confirm it collapses to p(x) either way:**
- If p(x) ≥ q(x): min(·) = q(x), max(·) = p(x)−q(x) → sum = q(x)+p(x)−q(x) = p(x) ✓
- If p(x) < q(x): min(·) = p(x), max(·) = 0 → sum = p(x)+0 = p(x) ✓

$$\boxed{P(\text{final}=x)=p(x)\quad\forall x}$$ ∎

**One-line takeaway:** the accept-step contributes the "overlap" between p and q, the resample-step contributes exactly the "gap" p has beyond q, and by Step 3 those two pieces are *guaranteed* to add back up to p — no matter how different p and q are.
